# Session handoff — 2026-08-11 evening

**Soul.** A long, productive evening in the meridian repo working in AUTO mode. The shape of the session was a slow tightening: PRs that wouldn't merge were unstuck, a missing TDD layer was discovered, the wireframe-vs-Anglar gap was quantified for the first time, and the first concrete page made the leap from wireframe HTML into a real routed Angular component with strict TDD discipline. Energy was practical and resolving: every question came with a fix, every fix came with a guardrail so the next one is cheaper.

**What We Did.** PR #3 (TDD enforcement + 19-primitives retrofit) shipped first after we smashed a vitest version-pin and an npm 11 peer-dependency regression in both `ci.yml` and `build.yml`. PR #2 had become stale after that merge; we cherry-picked the two unique files it carried (the `comment-policy` skill and its decision-log entry) onto a fresh `chore/pick-up-pr2-unique` branch as PR #4 and closed #2 with a comment so the history reads cleanly. Then we pivoted into the really interesting work: inventorying the 21 wireframe pages against the 19 primitives and the one routed Angular page (`/dashboard`). The result — 19/19 primitives cover what the wireframes use, but only 1/21 wireframes is implemented as a routed Angular page — reframed the question from "what components are missing" to "where are the pages."

**What We Found.** The first follow-up page, `/opportunities`, surfaced three real lessons that are now woven back into the project guardrails. The first is mechanical and easy to forget: page-level tests against existing primitives must read each primitive's template before writing — UiButtonComponent projects content via `<ng-content>`, so the host and the inner native `<button>` are two separate DOM elements, and naive textContent or aria-pressed assertions silently fail. The second is a limit in our own tooling: the `// TEST-COUPLED:` marker in `scripts/pre-commit.py` is currently a coarse "exempt all symbols in this file" flag, not per-symbol, so when a class has a public helper it didn't always work to add one marker; the right answer is almost always to write the unit test. The third is the easiest to repeat but the most annoying: running `npx vitest` from anywhere outside the `frontend/` directory silently falls back to npx's cached install and pulls a different vitest version than the lockfile, with confusing symptoms. All three are recorded as DISCOVERY entries inside `.agents/skills/tdd-enforcement/SKILL.md` so future agents won't relearn them the hard way.

**How to Continue.** master is at `74b08b0`, every gate green locally and on CI. The natural next move is `/executions` (lightbulb nav, second-highest traffic, structurally similar to `/opportunities` — filterable list with status tabs instead of pills). Follow the same rhythm: RED spec → GREEN impl → e2e playwright + screenshot → pre-commit (with a header on every new `.ts` plus a barrel `index.ts`) → PR → merge. Hold the line on the discipline — never production code without a failing test first, raw `<button class="btn ...">` over extending primitives when a primitive doesn't fit, and always `cd frontend/` before invoking vitest. Twenty pages remain after `/opportunities`; this session proved the cadence works, so the rest is repetition with focus.
